  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe weakening nor'easter is centered just west of Nova Scotia as of \nearly this afternoon but is still close enough to  support a few \nlight showers or patchy drizzle across western ME with enough low-\nlevel moisture still around. These chances will quickly fade toward \nevening with the system further exiting to the east through tonight \nand high pressure beginning to build into New England from the west. \nThere won't be much in the way of wind tonight, and given the \nwidespread recent rainfall, patchy fog is fair game for most the \narea and could be locally dense, especially in and around river \nvalleys. \n\nOnce fog clears up by mid-morning Wednesday, broad high pressure \nover the eastern CONUS will bring dry conditions and partly sunny \nweather for both Wednesday and Thursday. With the center of the high \nwell to the south, New England will see prevailing southwest winds \nbring warmer temperatures into the area. 850mb temps of \napproximately +9C to +10C supports highs mostly in the lower 70s\nWednesday, and +11C to +12C supports mid-upper 70s Thursday. \nThe exception is the southwest winds off the ocean keeping the \nMidcoast a few degrees cooler. Valley fog is possible Wednesday \nnight, but an upper disturbance may bring too much mid and high \nclouds. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nOn Friday, a shortwave looks to move through the \nnortheast. Ahead of this low Thursday night, a marginally unstable \nenvironment may allow for the development of showers. Despite benign \ninstability, the lack of forcing ahead of the front will likely keep \nshower activity isolated Thursday night and into Friday morning.\n\nBy Friday evening, the center of the low moves through southern New \nHampshire and could bring steady rain Friday night. Confidence is \nstill very low on rainfall amounts, with some model guidance \nsuggesting more rain than others. In addition, the placement of the \nrainfall too is another thing the models seem to struggle with too. \nThe 12Z run of the NAM today had the bulk of the rain north of the \nmountains, while the ECMWF deterministic had a more southerly \nplacement of said rain. Taking a look at the ECMWF ensemble, NH and \nwestern ME have a 50-80% chance of exceeding 0.1\" of rain and \nbetween a 15% and 30% chance of exceeding 0.5\" of rain. \n\nAfter the frontal passage Friday night, temperatures look to get \nmuch cooler for the weekend, with highs in the 60s. Radiational \ncooling Saturday night could also lead to some frost in locally \ncolder parts of the interior.",
